rt only, ReasonDeniedCrossTenant for everything else). The two matrices are STRUCTURALLY IDENTICAL (same role × action cross-product, same engine, same fixtures) but differ in resource scope (resourceIn(orgA) vs resourceIn(orgB)) and in the expected-verdict switch. Forward-applicable to ANY future security-verification story whose invariant has a "happy path × foreign-scope path" dual: BE-0398 (tenant isolation), BE-0404 (break-glass), BE-0407 (service desired-state goldens). The cross-tenant variant's switch MUST encode the engine's exact cross-tenant clause — guessing it from intuition (e.g. assuming Support is always allowed cross-tenant) fails the matrix because the engine ALSO checks the required cap via `roleCaps.has(CapSupport) && (required == CapRead || required == CapSupport)`. Verify by tracing engine.Decide BEFORE writing the test's expected-verdict switch. (3) `EXISTING-TEST-SATISFIES-RUN-FILTER` — unlike BE-0382 where the `-run TestOpenAPI` filter previously matched zero tests (the existing openapi tests were `TestBuild…` / `TestOperation…`), BE-0383's `-run TestPolicyMatrix` filter ALREADY matched the existing `TestPolicyMatrix` function in internal/controlplane/policy/policy_test.go. The story still adds value by (a) wiring the dedicated CI/verify.sh/CONTRIBUTING/SECURITY surfaces (the gate did not exist as a named gate before), (b) enriching the canonical test set with the cross-tenant matrix twin so the canonical-funcs pair is load-bearing, and (c) pinning the existence of both via the static test. Per-story knobs for siblings: (a) canonical command (BE-0383 uses `go test -run TestPolicyMatrix ./...` filter-narrowed; BE-0384 will use `go test -run TestQuotaConcurrency ./...`), (b) dedicated CI step name (BE-0383: "Policy matrix tests"), (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (BE-0383 inserted `# 9. Required: policy matrix tests` between BE-0382's `# 8` and the previous `# 9. Optional: govulncheck`, renumbering optionals 9→10/10→11/11→12/12→13 in the same edit — established by BE-0380's renumber pattern), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 13-entry substring set covering scope (internal/controlplane/policy) / determinism (deterministic fixtures) / actionable-failures (ReasonDeniedCrossTenant) / exceptions (CapSelf, CapSupport) / opt-in-external (YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY) / envelope-pinning (yalla.output.v1, yalla.error.v1) / matrix-pair (TestPolicyMatrix, TestPolicyMatrixDeniesCrossTenant) / redaction (redacted) / CI-cadence (Every push and PR) / self-locating gate filename (verification_suite_policy_matrix_static_test.go), (e) PRD requiredBackendCommands entry already present unchanged (BE-0383 did NOT add — the existing entry satisfies the matcher), (f) canonical reference test file path + matrix-pair func-name set + the `-run` filter prefix the canonical-funcs entries MUST satisfy. Load-bearing invariant: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:185:- Engine-only policy-matrix file pattern for CapSupport actions (BE-0285 admin.reconcile, applicable to admin.import / admin.read / admin.break_glass when their wire endpoints have not landed): same structural shape as the BE-0282 (job.cancel) CapDeploy template, but with three load-bearing flips: (1) role matrix collapses to one allow (Support) + five denies — the photo-negative of CapDeploy actions, (2) cross-tenant test flips Support from DENY (ReasonDeniedCrossTenant) to ALLOW (ReasonAllowedBySupport) because CapSupport sits INSIDE the engine's `required == CapRead || CapSupport` exception clause — the single most load-bearing distinction from BE-0279/0282 and the structural barrier against any future catalog drift that demoted admin.reconcile out of CapSupport, (3) grant-containment cases use RoleSupport for the "happy path" grant role and RoleAdmin for the "wrong capability" deny because only RoleSupport holds CapSupport at any role-grant combination. Resource Kind is KindOrganization with only Scope.OrganizationID pinned (admin actions operate on the source-of-truth view of an entire organization, no project/env/svc leg), so every project/env/service-scoped grant denies via ReasonDeniedOutOfScope (one-way covers()) — admin actions are unreachable through any scoped grant other than org-level Support. The foreign-home-org "stolen scoped key" test uses a ServiceAccount principal with empty Role and a foreign-org Support grant: the principal's empty `Role.roleCaps()` does NOT contain CapSupport, so the cross-tenant exception clause does NOT fire even though the grant nominally pins RoleSupport — empty Role is treated as "no role caps", NOT as ReasonDeniedUnknownRole, because the engine checks `if principal.Role != "" && !roleOK` before denying unknown roles. Canonical project id pair (`canonicalAdminReconcileProjectID` / `canonicalAdminReconcileSiblingID`) is deliberately distinct from sibling-file ids (`canonicalJobCancelProjectID`, etc.) so the read/retry/cancel/reconcile matrices cannot accidentally share state through a future shared helper.
ralph/progress.txt:237:- Authorization is internal/controlplane/policy: policy.Engine is a pure, total decision function — Decide(Principal, Action, Resource) -> Decision{Allow, Reason} (stable Reason codes); Authorize/AuthorizeCtx map denials onto typed apierr (Unauthenticated for a missing principal, Forbidden otherwise). Every endpoint maps to exactly one policy.Action; the action lives in policy's defaultActionCatalog (the authoritative requiredAction -> Capability map) — add new actions there or via policy.WithAction. Roles confer Capability sets (self/read/deploy/write/admin/owner/support), not individual actions; six built-ins are fixed, custom roles resolve through a CustomRoleResolver hook. Scoped Grants narrow/widen authority within an org via Scope containment over Organization->Project->Environment->Service; cross-tenant resources are denied by construction except support reads/break-glass. The principal travels on the request context via policy.WithPrincipal/PrincipalFromContext — never thread it through signatures. The store.Authorizer port is satisfied by this engine; store still never imports policy. TestPolicyMatrix exhaustively covers all built-in roles x catalogued actions.
ralph/progress.txt:279:- Pagination contract package (BE-0037, internal/controlplane/pagination): every customer-facing list endpoint composes the shared pagination.Params + pagination.Cursor + pagination.Page[T] envelope rather than reimplementing limit/cursor/sort/filter parsing. ParseParams(url.Values, ParseOptions) parses ?limit= ?cursor= ?sort= ?direction= ?filter.<k>= with stable apierr.InvalidInput (E_INVALID_INPUT) violations whose Reason text NEVER echoes the submitted value (only classification + accepted range — protects against reflection-style content channels), accumulates multiple violations into one envelope, enforces the package MaxLimit=200 ceiling even when an endpoint misconfigures its own, and rejects a cursor whose embedded Sort/Direction disagrees with the request as a stable 400 "fetch the first page again" instead of silently serving overlapping pages. Cursor is base64url-encoded JSON {v: "yalla.cursor.v1", p: Position, s: Sort, d: Direction} — opaque to clients, deterministic (same input -> same encoding so HTTP fixtures diff byte-for-byte), URL-safe (RawURLEncoding, no '+/='), and DOES NOT carry an organization_id / tenant / principal field by contract (a TestDecodeCursorTenantIsolationCannotBeBypassed test guards this so adding one is impossible without breaking the suite — tenant isolation stays the persistence layer's responsibility, cursor only encodes a within-set position). Page[T any] is the wire shape: Items always non-nil (BuildPage guarantees []T{} for empty), NextCursor omitempty (empty string == end of stream — agents do NOT compare len(Items) to limit), TotalEstimate *int64 omitempty (nil = "not cheap to compute", endpoints must NOT fabricate a value). BuildPage uses the over-fetch strategy: store fetches Limit+1 rows, BuildPage trims to Limit and encodes next_cursor from the last retained row's position via a PositionFunc[T] adapter. Cursor stability under inserts is proven end-to-end by TestCursorIsStableUnderInserts: seed N rows, page once, insert NEWER rows after the cursor boundary, prove page 2 still returns exactly the remaining originals (no duplicates, no skips, no newly-inserted rows leaking onto page 2 because the cursor is a strict upper bound on (sort_key DESC, id DESC)). Future list endpoints replace their bespoke parseXxxLimit / list payload struct with one ParseParams call + a BuildPage projection — the existing GET /v1/organizations/{org_id}/audit-events (BE-0103) and break_glass list are pre-pagination-contract and should migrate in their own stories.
